-- Let GHC take part in cabal's parallelism. Cabal creates a system semaphore
-- with as many tokens as -j allows and passes it to GHC with -jsem, so GHC can
-- use whatever parallelism cabal-install is not using itself. Without this, a
-- single-package build such as `cabal build cabal-install` compiles its modules
-- serially no matter what -j says, because -j only schedules packages.
--
-- GHC advertises the semaphore protocol it speaks in `ghc --info`. Cabal speaks
-- v2, and a compiler reporting no version at all is assumed to be v1. Compilers
-- without v2 make `semaphore: True` inert *and* warn about the version mismatch
-- on every build, so enable it only where v2 is expected:
--
--   * 9.10.4 and later in the 9.10 series
--   * 9.12.5 and later in the 9.12 series
--   * after 9.14.1, which covers the 9.14.1.20260728 prerelease that was the
--     first compiler seen to report v2, and 9.14.2 onwards
--   * everything newer, including 10.*
--
-- Confirmed by hand: 9.14.1.20260728 reports "Semaphore version" 2 and cabal
-- passes -jsem; 9.14.1 and 9.10.3 report no such field and cabal warns.
--
-- `jobs` is set alongside because the semaphore gets its token count from it and
-- `jobs` defaults to 1, which would make a one-token semaphore and no parallelism
-- at all. The generated user config normally carries `jobs: $ncpus` already, but
-- a project should not depend on that. An explicit -j on the command line still
-- wins over this, so CI passing `-j 2` is unaffected. Both settings sit inside the
-- condition so that nothing changes on compilers that cannot use the semaphore.
if impl(ghc >= 9.10.4 && < 9.11) || impl(ghc >= 9.12.5 && < 9.13) || impl(ghc > 9.14.1)
  jobs: $ncpus
  semaphore: True
